  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nLow level stratus has begun to thin, becoming scattered to few this \nafternoon as slightly warmer, light winds aloft erode it from \nthe top down. This evening and overnight an upper trough will \npass through central Canada toward the Great Lakes region. This \nwill drag a diffuse surface boundary and narrow band of slightly\nhigher moisture through the region. Weak instability and WAA in\nthe moist layer will likely result in low stratus formation \nafter midnight tonight. Subsiding air aloft will work to lower \nthe stratus to very near the surface. At the same time cooling \nsurface temperatures approach the dew points and patchy dense \nfog may also form. The combination of low stratus/fog could \nresult in visibility decreasing to 1 mile or less at times. REFS\nguidance indicates a medium probability of 50-70% in low \nvisibility through mid-morning Saturday. There is a very low \nprobability (<15%) of freezing fog, mostly north of I-90 where \ntemperatures are projected to fall to or just below freezing. \nThis could result in scattered slippery spots on roads. Please \nuse caution and be prepared for rapidly changing road \nconditions. South of I-90 lows are expected to be in the low to \nmid 30s. There is some uncertainty with overnight lows due to \nuncertainty in stratus coverage and thickness. If it is very \nthin temperatures could be a degree or two colder than \nforecasted. \n\nClouds slowly dissipate Saturday morning as light but warmer south-\nsouthwesterly winds flow into the region. By Saturday afternoon \npartly to mostly sunny skies are expected as highs climb into the \nmid 50s to low 60s. Late Saturday afternoon into the evening a \nsurface low located just southwest of the Hudson Bay will drag a \ncold front through the upper Plains. This will turn winds northwest \nfor the overnight hours. Lows will fall to the mid to upper 30s. An \nupper ridge moves over the Plains during the day Sunday, turning \nwinds to the south by the afternoon. Highs will climb into the 50s \nand low 60s. \n\nMonday and through the middle of next week the pattern aloft will be \n\"wavy\". However, lack of moisture at the surface and midlevels keep \nthe forecast dry. Highs will be warmest Monday in the low to mid \n60s. Temperatures Tuesday through Thursday will be cooler in the \n50s, but still above average for this time of year (upper 40s). \nWednesday night into Thursday bring the next chance for \nprecipitation, but details are vague at this time.",
